<Concept title="Engineering Policy (Beta)" section="Spec">
  <p>Current review baselines: <a href={siteUrl('/fidelity')}>fidelity and quality grades</a> · <a href={siteUrl('/states/combat-roster')}>combat roster identity and coverage</a>.</p>
  <p>The key words <strong>MUST</strong>, <strong>MUST NOT</strong>, <strong>SHOULD</strong>, and <strong>MAY</strong> are used as in RFC 2119. These are the working rules for all Beta implementation work; they exist so the Alpha cleanup cost (#215's 50-item audit) doesn't repeat.</p>

  <h2>Class hierarchies: behavior in bases, leaves earn their existence</h2>
  <p>Shared behavior <strong>MUST</strong> live in a base class with a clear interface — <code>Enemy</code> owns AI dispatch, status handling, damage intake; <code>WolfEnemy</code> / <code>SmallEnemy</code>-style subclasses exist only where <em>behavior</em> genuinely diverges (a different FSM, a different movement model). This applies to enemies, player classes, weapons, and shops alike.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Data variation is not behavior.</strong> Stats, models, elements, spawn tables stay in resources (<code>EnemyData</code>, <code>WeaponData</code>) — a new enemy that only differs in numbers <strong>MUST</strong> be a new resource, not a new script (per <a href={siteUrl('/states/enemies')}>/states/enemies</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>Leaf classes are one-offs that earn it.</strong> A PR adding a leaf class <strong>MUST</strong> state why the behavior can't be expressed in the base (a hook, a parameter, a resource field). "It was faster" is the answer we're guarding against.</li>
    <li><strong>When the second similar thing appears, extract the base first.</strong> Don't ship sibling copies and dedup later — that was Alpha's pattern and it cost a milestone to undo.</li>
    <li><strong>Every hierarchy gets a spec page.</strong> Expected behavior of the base contract (which methods subclasses override, which invariants hold) lives under <a href={siteUrl('/hierarchies')}>/hierarchies</a> — <a href={siteUrl('/hierarchies/enemy')}>Enemy</a>, <a href={siteUrl('/hierarchies/weapon')}>Weapon</a>, and the <a href={siteUrl('/hierarchies/shop-screen')}>shop-screen composition contract</a> are defined; new hierarchies add a page before/with implementation.</li>
    <li><strong>Known exception — shop screens.</strong> Lazily-loaded 2D screens <strong>MUST NOT</strong> use cross-script <code>class_name</code> inheritance: it breaks the Android export (<code>Could not resolve class "ShopBase"</code> — see <code>docs/shop-dedup.md</code>). Screens reuse via preloaded static helpers (<code>ShopNav</code>, <code>ShopUI</code>) — same grouping goal, composition mechanism.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Tech debt: blocked by default, loud when deliberate</h2>
  <p>The CI ratchet (<code>code_graph.py --check</code>) fails any PR introducing <em>new</em> duplication, dead code, complexity, or coupling. Accepting debt by growing a baseline is possible but <strong>MUST</strong> be acknowledged: the baseline-debt guard fails any PR that increases an accepted-debt count unless a commit carries a <code>Debt-Accepted: &lt;reason&gt;</code> trailer.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Baseline <em>decreases</em> are wins and pass silently — shrink freely.</li>
    <li>Deliberate one-off debt belongs at the <strong>end</strong> of beta, if at all. A mid-beta <code>Debt-Accepted</code> is a prompt to ask <em>why now?</em> — the answer goes in the trailer.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Testing: two layers for anything that moves</h2>
  <p>Every gameplay mechanic — and <em>especially</em> attacks, timing windows, and frame-dependent behavior — <strong>MUST</strong> ship with both layers:</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Layer</th><th>What</th><th>Catches</th></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Unit</strong></td><td>Seeded test in <code>test_runner.gd</code> pinning the math / window classification / state transition (the <code>test_element_status</code> / <code>test_player_states</code> pattern)</td><td>formula and logic regressions, deterministically</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Post-build</strong></td><td>An autopilot probe — a matrix phase, smoke script, or sanity checkpoint that exercises the mechanic in the running game</td><td>integration breakage units can't see (the #335 lesson: the unit suite was green while the autopilot hung)</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>A combat increment whose plan has only one layer is not done being planned. The unit layer is the merge gate in CI; the post-build layer runs in the sanity check (every merge) and the full matrix (every combat-behavior PR).</p>

  <h2>Enforced by</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>scripts/tools/code_graph.py --check</code> — the four-way ratchet (CI, every PR).</li>
    <li><code>scripts/tools/check_baseline_debt.py</code> — the debt-acknowledgment guard (CI, every PR).</li>
    <li><code>scripts/tools/autoplay/merge_gate.sh</code> — blocks merges without a green sanity run for the exact HEAD.</li>
    <li><code>scripts/tools/autoplay/run_regression_matrix.sh</code> — the full post-build layer (dev-box, before combat merges).</li>
  </ul>
  <h2 id="mirrored-repeat-porting">Mirrored UV baking into native Godot materials</h2>
  <p>Target contract for <a href="https://github.com/kion-dgl/psz-godot/issues/381">#381</a>: static mirrored materials <strong>MUST</strong> bake their wrapping into asset UVs and use <code>StandardMaterial3D</code>, so lighting and material features remain owned by Godot. The existing mirror shaders are a migration fallback, not completion of this issue. This section defines the intended migration. The <a href={siteUrl('/tools/mirror-bake/')}>Three.js Mirror UV Bake lab</a> implements the first comparison prototype; the local field-stage pipeline is implemented in <code>tools/stage_mirror_bake</code>, with pack publication pending.</p>
  <p>The bake <strong>MUST</strong> apply authored UV scale and offset. A geometry-fold bake MUST split triangles at every crossed integer mirror boundary and reverse alternate tiles on each mirrored axis, including negative coordinates. When a reflected texture period is used instead, the bake MAY map the continuous transformed UV to half its value on each mirrored axis; ordinary repeat then reproduces the same pattern without subdivision and preserves the original topology, skin weights and continuous mip derivatives. Folding only existing vertices is insufficient for triangles crossing a boundary. New vertices <strong>MUST</strong> preserve interpolated surface attributes and material assignments; positions, silhouettes and animation behavior must remain intact.</p>
  <p>The output <strong>MUST</strong> preserve repeat/clamp behavior on non-mirrored axes and avoid opposite-edge bleeding at mirror folds under linear filtering. The implementation must resolve sampler and texture-edge handling, including mixed wrap axes and mipmaps, before claiming visual equivalence. The shader's half-texel clamp is a reference behavior, not a license to shrink every triangle's UVs and distort texture sampling.</p>
  <p>The independent Godot proof of concept lives in <code>tools/mirror_uv_bake</code>. Its right-hand candidate <strong>MUST</strong> use only <code>StandardMaterial3D</code>. For the mixed mirror/repeat case, it MAY expand the texture with a reflected tile on each mirrored axis and map folded UVs into the first half of that axis. Ordinary repeat sampling then supplies the correct edge texels while leaving the other axis repeating. This costs 2× texture pixels per mirrored axis (4× for both); it is a proof-of-concept tradeoff, not a blanket asset policy. The comparison <strong>MUST</strong> expose texture dimensions, native material identity, linked cameras, lighting and filtering, and produce numerical render evidence. Shader-free texture placement, native lighting response and a human review gate precede any bulk port.</p>
  <p>Baked assets <strong>MUST</strong> carry an explicit bake version and settings identity so loaders do not reapply scale, offset or mirror shaders. Re-running the pipeline from source <strong>MUST</strong> be deterministic. Static mirrored surfaces <strong>MUST NOT</strong> retain a custom shader solely for mirrored wrapping. Independently animated effects require a separate material decision; their existence must not force static surfaces onto a custom shader.</p>
  <p>Validation <strong>MUST</strong> cover negative tiles, odd/even folds, multiple crossed boundaries, mixed axes, fractional scales, offsets and preserved vertex attributes. Render comparisons <strong>MUST</strong> check seams and texture placement against the mirrored reference, and Godot validation <strong>MUST</strong> verify that migrated surfaces use native materials and respond to lighting. Original source assets must remain recoverable until the migrated assets are verified.</p>
  <p>Stage rollout contract (#381): static mirror rows across all field areas (Valley, Wetlands, Snowfield, Makara, Paru, Arca, Shrine and Tower) are eligible. Each area MUST retain its authored lighting slot and imported mipmap availability. The inventory MUST record every skipped mirror surface with a reason. City scenes use a separate material pipeline and MUST be inventoried separately before migration. Hidden, scrolling, strip-animated, blended-effect, morph and non-triangle inputs MUST be excluded or reported. Original GLBs and texture-fix rows MUST remain intact. Generated variants MUST preserve transforms, topology, skin data, material names and RGB vertex tint; vertex alpha MUST be made opaque only on migrated surfaces because the former shader ignores it. The field material pass MUST recognize verified bake metadata, avoid reapplying scale/offset, and honor the existing lit-surface policy. Variants MUST be shared by content and wrap settings, record source/settings identities, and fall back to the original shader if absent or stale. Seeded sampling tests and an all-stage material audit and representative render sweep for every converted area variant precede gameplay review and pack publication. Valley free-roam appearance has owner approval; other areas require gameplay review.</p>
  <p>Material settings <strong>MUST</strong> be established per model. Storybook settings describe project authoring, not automatically original DS evidence. Needle Trap (<code>o0c_needle</code>), Bear Trap (<code>o0c_torabasami</code>) and Clay Trap/claymore (<code>o0c_clay1</code>) must not receive a blanket shared material solely because they are traps. City (<code>score:city</code>), free roam (<code>score:free-roam</code>) and traps (<code>score:traps</code>) retain their current grades; migrated-asset playtesting is pending.</p>

  <h2>Stage texture strips</h2>
  <p>Texture-fix rows MAY specify <code>framesY</code> and <code>fps</code> for a vertical strip. The field MUST sample one frame at a time, advance top-to-bottom, and loop. The mesh MUST already address the first row (V = 0–1/framesY). Playback MUST preserve that UV span and add frame/framesY, rather than scaling V again; filtering MUST NOT bleed into adjacent rows. The Coliseum's <code>s00_1_set3</code> uses eight frames at 8 fps.</p>
  <h2>Unified static site — October 8</h2>
  <p>The repository root owns one Astro application, package manifest and lockfile. Run npm install, npm run dev, npm test and npm run build from the root. Existing React tools live under web/src and are hosted by the Astro homepage; spec/src contains the document routes. Folder names no longer describe separate applications.</p>
  <p>GitHub Pages serves dist/site under /psz-godot/. Tools have ordinary /tools/ URLs with prerendered deep-link pages. Old hash URLs redirect from the homepage. Spec navigation uses the same base. All resource URLs use the shared asset helper; local editor save/export plugins run only under astro dev. Development file access is limited to assets/.</p>
  <p>The site has no report endpoint, report viewer, telemetry capture or Node server. DigitalOcean mirrors static assets and the Godot Web export at https://psz.onl/game/. Existing stored reports are not deleted by this migration. Retiring the old live Node service and redirecting legacy document URLs is a separate operational cutover after this static build is reviewed.</p>
</Concept>
